<p><a name="fn10"></a>10. Banks receive ratings that quantify their safety and soundness based on supervisory exams. Ratings evaluate banks' performance on key risks, including their capital and liquidity positions and management quality. <a href="#f10">Return to text</a></p>

<p><a name="fn11"></a>11. See, for example, key findings that the Management component of CAMELS ratings has significant predictive power for future bank risk/performance. Lewis Gaul and Jonathan Jones, <a href="https://www.occ.treas.gov/publications-and-resources/publications/economics/working-papers-banking-perf-reg/economic-working-camels-ratings.html">"CAMELS Ratings and Their Information Content,"</a> OCC Working Paper WP-2021-01 (Office of the Comptroller of the Currency, January 2021). <a href="#f11">Return to text</a></p>

<p><a name="fn12"></a>12. Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/bcreg20251105a.htm">"Federal Reserve Board Finalizes Changes to Its Supervisory Rating Framework for Large Bank Holding Companies,"</a> press release, November 5, 2025. <a href="#f12">Return to text</a></p>

<p><a name="fn13"></a>13. <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/barr-statement-20251105.htm">"Statement on Large Financial Institution Rating Framework by Governor Michael S. Barr,"</a> press release, November 5, 2025. <a href="#f13">Return to text</a></p>

<p><a name="fn14"></a>14. CAMELS evaluates six components: Capital Adequacy, Asset Quality, Management, Earnings, Liquidity, and Sensitivity to Market Risk. Each component is rated on a scale from 1 to 5, and together they form the institution's composite rating, which supervisors rely on to evaluate safety and soundness. See Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/boarddocs/srletters/1996/sr9638.htm">"Uniform Financial Institutions Rating System,"</a> SR letter 96-38 (December 27, 1996). <a href="#f14">Return to text</a></p>

<p><a name="fn15"></a>15. See Gaul and Jones, "CAMELS Ratings and Their Information Content" (finding that Management rating is most strongly correlated with composite rating of all CAMELS components and has significant predictive power for future bank performance and risk measures). <a href="#f15">Return to text</a></p>

<p><a name="fn16"></a>16. Unsafe and unsound practices would be defined under Section 8 of the FDIA as any act, practice, or failure to act that deviates from generally accepted standards of prudent banking and either causes material harm to an institution's financial condition or, if continued, would be likely to do so or pose a material risk to the Deposit Insurance Fund. See <a href="https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/10/30/2025-19711/unsafe-or-unsound-practices-matters-requiring-attention">https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/10/30/2025-19711/unsafe-or-unsound-practices-matters-requiring-attention</a>. <a href="#f16">Return to text</a></p>

<p><a name="fn17"></a>17. Federal Housing Finance Agency: Office of Inspector General, <a href="https://www.fhfaoig.gov/Content/Files/EVL-2018-002_Redacted.pdf" target="_blank"><em>FHFA Requires the Enterprises' Internal Audit Functions to Validate Remediation of Serious Deficiencies but Provides No Guidance and Imposes No Preconditions on Examiners' Use of That Validation Work</em> (PDF)</a> (FHFA: OIG, March 2018). <a href="#f17">Return to text</a></p>

<p><a name="fn18"></a>18. Tobias Adrian, Marina Moretti, Ana Carvalho, Hee Kyong Chon, Fabiana Melo, Katharine Seal, &amp; and Jay Surti, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5089/9798400253782.001">"Good Supervision: Lessons from the Field,"</a> IMF Working Paper WP/No. 23/181 (International Monetary Fund, September 2023): 18–20. <a href="#f18">Return to text</a></p>

<p><a name="fn19"></a>19. See <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/boardmeetings/enhanced-transparency-and-public-accountability-proposal-frn.pdf">https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/boardmeetings/enhanced-transparency-and-public-accountability-proposal-frn.pdf</a>. <a href="#f19">Return to text</a></p>

<p><a name="fn20"></a>20. <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/barr-statement-20251024.htm">"Statement on Proposals to Enhance the Transparency and Public Accountability Of the Board's Stress Testing Framework By Governor Michael S. Barr,"</a> press release, October 24, 2025. <a href="#f20">Return to text</a></p>

<p><a name="fn21"></a>21. "Horizontal reviews and benchmarking provide a cross-sectoral perspective of risks, enable a deeper review of certain topics and promote consistency in the exercise of expert judgment...Horizontal reviews that focus on a specific issue across banks are increasingly used for a proactive, forward-looking supervisory approach." See <a href="https://www.bis.org/bcbs/publ/wp45.pdf">https://www.bis.org/bcbs/publ/wp45.pdf</a> at 2. <a href="#f21">Return to text</a></p>

<p><a name="fn22"></a>22. <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/supervisionreg/understanding-federal-reserve-supervision.htm">"Understanding Federal Reserve Supervision,"</a> Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, last modified April 27, 2023. <a href="#f22">Return to text</a></p>

<p><a name="fn23"></a>23. Katanga Johnson and Weihua Li, "Trump Cuts Thousands of Wall Street Cops While Markets Swing," <em>Bloomberg</em>, May 7, 2025, https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-05-07/trump-s-layoffs-cut-more-than-2-300-from-us-bank-and-markets-regulators?sref=zNmRQ0gk. <a href="#f23">Return to text</a></p>

<p><a name="fn24"></a>24. Michael S. Barr (2025), "Booms and Busts and the Regulatory Cycle." <a href="#f24">Return to text</a></p>
                     
